---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

// Tombstone. Profiles were retired in favour of blueprints; the URL stays alive
// because it was published and people link to published URLs. The matching
// tombstone in the repository is docs/profiles.md.
---

<Base
  title="Profiles — replaced by blueprints — AI Standard"
  description="The three adoption profiles were replaced by blueprints. This page explains what changed and where the material went."
  schemaType="WebPage"
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Profiles were replaced by blueprints</h1>

  <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl opacity-80">
    This page used to describe three adoption profiles — OSS-only, AI-only, and Combined — sharing one
    repository and one version stream. That model is retired. The URL stays because it was published, and
    removing published URLs is how documentation becomes untrustworthy.
  </p>

  <section class="mt-12 flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">What was wrong with profiles</h2>
    <p class="opacity-80">
      A profile answered <em>which bundle of files applies to a project like mine</em>. It turned out to be the
      wrong question in three ways.
    </p>
    <ul class="flex list-disc flex-col gap-2 pl-6 opacity-80">
      <li>
        <strong>It asked you to classify yourself first.</strong> Before you could take anything you had to
        decide which of three kinds of project you were — and people who are unsure pick wrong, or pick the
        largest.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The combined profile was the union of the other two</strong>, which meant the real answer to
        most questions was "take everything", and taking everything is how a repository ends up describing a
        structure that does not exist.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing said what a bundle was for.</strong> A file list is not a reason. It cannot tell you
        when <em>not</em> to take it, which is the part that saves you work.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">What replaced them</h2>
    <p class="opacity-80">
      A blueprint is material you copy into your own project, cut by <strong>trigger</strong> — something that
      has already happened to you — rather than by the kind of project you think you have. Each one has to
      answer four questions before it may exist: what it solves, what you get, <em>when not to use it</em>, and
      what it has actually been run against.
    </p>
    <p class="opacity-80">
      The last two are the ones profiles never had. A blueprint nobody has run is a suggestion, and it has to
      say so.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
      <a
        href={`${base}use-cases`}
        class="rounded-md bg-[var(--color-accent)] px-5 py-2.5 font-medium text-[var(--color-on-accent)] no-underline hover:opacity-90"
      >
        The triggers, and what each one takes
      </a>
      <a
        href={`${base}docs/blueprints`}
        class="rounded-md border border-black/15 px-5 py-2.5 font-medium no-underline hover:bg-black/5 dark:border-white/15 dark:hover:bg-white/5"
      >
        What a blueprint is
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="mt-12 text-sm opacity-60">
    The repository keeps a matching tombstone at <a href={`${base}docs/profiles`}>docs/profiles</a>, and its
    self-conformance check fails the build if the retired vocabulary reappears as current usage anywhere else.
  </p>
</Base>
